This challenge is **normal** and actually a sign you’re moving from **UI clone developer → product-thinking developer**.

Most beginners design screens first, then later realize:

> “Wait… what data actually powers this?”

That pain is where real app building starts.

Your issue is:

> **You designed UI before defining the financial model (business logic).**

You built the “skin” before the “brain.”

Let’s fix that properly.

---

# First: Define the core financial model

Everything in Spendly should derive from these base values:

```text
Monthly Budget
Income
Expenses
Savings
Categories
Current Date
```

Example realistic dataset:

```text
Monthly Budget = ₦300,000
Income = ₦350,000
Current Month Expenses = ₦180,000
Savings = ₦70,000
Today = May 22
Month = 31 days
```

Categories:

```text
Food = ₦55,000
Transport = ₦25,000
Shopping = ₦40,000
Bills = ₦35,000
Entertainment = ₦15,000
Health = ₦10,000
```

Once this exists:

> every UI number becomes computable.

---

# 1. HOME PAGE

## A. Monthly Budget

This is:

```text
User planned spending limit
```

Example:

```text
₦300,000
```

User sets this manually.

---

## B. Amount Spent

Formula:

```text
sum(all expenses this month)
```

Example:

```text
55k + 25k + 40k + 35k + 15k + 10k
= ₦180,000
```

---

## C. Amount Left

Formula:

```text
budget - spent
```

Example:

```text
300,000 - 180,000
= ₦120,000 left
```

---

## D. Daily Average

This should NOT be random.

Formula:

```text
spent ÷ elapsedDays
```

Example:

Today = 22nd

```text
180,000 ÷ 22
= ₦8,181/day
```

Why elapsed days?

Because user hasn’t lived future days yet.

Bad calculation:

```text
180,000 ÷ 31
```

That lies.

---

## E. Days Left

Formula:

```text
daysInMonth - currentDay
```

Example:

May:

```text
31 - 22
= 9 days left
```

---

# Quick Insight Section

This should show **comparison trend**.

---

## Expense insight

Example:

```text
Expenses ↑ 18%
vs last month
```

Formula:

```text
((currentMonth - lastMonth)
÷ lastMonth) × 100
```

Example:

April:

```text
150k
```

May:

```text
180k
```

Calculation:

```text
(180k - 150k)
÷ 150k × 100

= 20%
```

Meaning:

```text
Spent 20% more
```

---

## Savings insight

Formula:

```text
income - expenses
```

Example:

```text
350k - 180k
= ₦170k
```

Then compare with last month.

Example:

```text
Savings ↑ 12%
```

---

# 2. ANALYTICS PAGE

## Amount Spent

Same as home:

```text
sum(month expenses)
```

---

## AVG/DAY + percentage

Average:

```text
spent ÷ elapsedDays
```

Percentage:

Should represent:

### spending pace vs budget

Formula:

```text
(spent ÷ budget) × 100
```

Example:

```text
180k ÷ 300k
= 60%
```

Meaning:

```text
60% of budget used
```

---

## Budget in Percentage

This is basically:

### Remaining budget %

Formula:

```text
(left ÷ budget) × 100
```

Example:

```text
120k ÷ 300k
= 40%
```

Display:

```text
40% budget remaining
```

---

# Spending by Category (Bar Chart)

YES:

This should be **SPENT**, not saved.

Because people spend per category.

Example:

```text
Food          ₦55k
Shopping      ₦40k
Bills         ₦35k
Transport     ₦25k
Entertainment ₦15k
Health        ₦10k
```

Chart formula:

```text
categorySpent ÷ totalSpent
```

Example:

Food:

```text
55k ÷ 180k
= 30.5%
```

That determines bar height.

---

## Category amount section below chart

Show:

```text
Spent amount
```

Better format:

```text
Food & Dining
₦55,000 • 30%
```

Not savings.

Savings per category is weird UX.

People budget per category.

